otal": 5.93, "baseline_margin": 5.66, "analyst_adjustment": -1.66, "key_factors": ["Recent polls show a near-tied race, with an average margin under two points, well below the state's Democratic lean", "Betting markets price Kotek at roughly 76% and most analysts rate the race Lean D", "Kotek's incumbency and Oregon's D+8 fundamentals still favor Democrats even as polls tighten", "A third-party candidate and voter unease keep the electorate unusually volatile"], "rationale": "Polls show a far tighter contest than Oregon's partisan lean alone would imply, with the recent survey average near a tie and Drazan ahead in two September polls. Kotek's incumbency, the state's fundamentals, and market pricing around 76% argue she remains favored, so a modest edge above the polling average is warranted.", "watch": "A late shift among independent voters, or uneven turnout between the Portland metro and the rest of the state, could tip a margin this narrow either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Tina Kotek (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Oregon has voted comfortably Democratic in recent cycles, but Governor Tina Kotek's re-election bid against Republican Christine Drazan is running much closer than the state's usual margins. Recent surveys show a near-tied contest: an early October poll put Kotek up four, while September surveys from DHM Research had Drazan narrowly ahead. Analysts mostly rate the race Lean D, with two calling it Likely D and RealClearPolitics a Tossup, and betting markets give Kotek roughly a three-in-four chance.\n\nKotek benefits from incumbency, a D+8 state, and a third candidate on the ballot who may fragment opposition to her. Drazan has campaigned as her own person, and the final debate coverage centered on development and voter unease. The race appears to be Kotek's to lose, but the polling margin is thin enough that small shifts in turnout or late persuasion could matter a great deal.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9